              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing station map details**\n\nI’m looking at the station label around the Court St area on the map. It seems like the black grouped symbols indicate a transfer; the black capsules (R)(3)(2)(4) suggest they all serve the same station. Court St seems to be linked to Borough Hall by a beige dotted walking route. I notice the R yellow line enters Court St, while the red 2/3 lines run vertically through it, and line 4 runs diagonally. So, Court St only services R, while 2, 3, and 4 connects nearby.**Evaluating station transfer connections**\n\nIt looks like the capsule notation suggests multiple routes allow for free transfers between stations, rather than indicating a single station. The grouping of R, 2, 3, and 4 indicates that you can transfer freely from Court St (R) to Borough Hall (2, 3, 4). To get to Atlantic quickly, I can take line 4 from Borough Hall to Nevins—then the Atlantic line with two stops. There’s also a direct transfer option when reaching Atlantic for accessing Q and other lines.**Analyzing DeKalb station services**\n\nLooking at the map, DeKalb station only has the Q line stopping there, while the D and N lines pass through without stopping. The Q line is marked in yellow, and I'm unsure where the orange D line fits in, as it may not stop at DeKalb. DeKalb serves B, Q, and R lines on weekends; using Q from Atlantic seems logical. I could transfer from 4 at Borough Hall to Atlantic, and then ride Q to DeKalb. I wonder if only Q operates between these two stations.",
